// English Zipf table (log10 occurrences per billion words; "the" 7.7, "home" 5.9, "tub" 4.1,
// "orrery" absent): every word with blended Zipf >= 3.0 — the whole band common enough in
// narrative/conversational English to be a dubious trigger key; absence from the map means rare
// (full suggestion weight). PRIMARY source is SUBTLEX-US (51M words of film/TV subtitles) because
// lorebook prose is narrative register — the web/Wikipedia-blended wordfreq list overrated
// technical vocabulary ("prototype" 3.8 there, 3.5 spoken; "jubilee" 3.4 vs 2.9). wordfreq acts
// as a discounted veto, z = max(subtlex, wordfreq - 0.6), because SUBTLEX is 2004-era and rates
// time-shifted vocabulary falsely rare ("texts" 2.8 in subtitles, 4.3 on the web).
// ponytail: 0.6 register discount eyeballed off measured gaps (corporation 0.7, omega 0.6,
// consultant 0.6); retune against real books, not the lists.
// Stored quantised to 0.1 as decizipf:words bands. commonwords.js keeps the rank-ordered
// top-2000 for the pruner's binary flag; this table exists because rank order loses the shape of
// the distribution (Zipf falls as ~1/rank).
//
// POS sets, from SUBTLEX Dom_PoS + Percentage_dom_PoS, all 74k words eligible regardless of z
// (rare words are exactly what the frequency table misses):
//   POS_VA_STRICT — dominant Verb/Adverb at >= 95%: pure verbs ("watched" 1.0, "steal" .98,
//     "accepts" .99). Safe to kill ANYWHERE in a phrase — an interior or leading pure verb marks
//     a clause fragment ("watched jeffrey", "jeffrey watched teddy").
//   POS_VA — >= 85%: adds heads-only cases ("fallen" .89). Participle-adjectives that lead real
//     noun phrases (broken .62, burning .62, twisted .57, hidden .67, stolen .71) sit below both
//     bars and are never killed.
//   POS_ADJ — dominant Adjective at >= 85% ("voracious"/"pragmatic"/"gravelly" ~1.0): bad as a
//     BARE unigram key (attributive words over-fire detached from their noun) while free to lead
//     or ride in phrases ("voracious reader"). Ambiguous adjective/nouns fall under the bar
//     ("giant" .75, "cool" .84) and "randy" tags Name.
// Rebuild: SUBTLEX-US "frequency list with PoS" text file (crr.ugent.be, mirrored on
// web.archive.org) -> z=log10(SUBTLWF)+3 folded lowercase keeping max, unioned with wordfreq
// iter_wordlist('en','large') at z-0.6, floor 3.0; POS sets from Dom_PoS/Percentage_dom_PoS.
export const ZIPF_EN = (() => {
    const m = new Map();
    for (const row of PACKED.split(';')) {
        const i = row.indexOf(':'), z = Number(row.slice(0, i)) / 10;
        for (const w of row.slice(i + 1).split(' ')) m.set(w, z);
    }
    return m;
})();
export const POS_VA_STRICT = new Set(VA95.split(' '));
export const POS_VA = new Set([...POS_VA_STRICT, ...VA85.split(' ')]);
export const POS_ADJ = new Set(ADJ85.split(' '));
